  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "p1UlC3SOyZ7Y0WXAFzMYKd3sFJs=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-17T09:58:37.025Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`IfcRelSequence` is a standardised, typed task dependency inside the IFC schema, with predecessor logic (`FINISH_START`, `START_START`, `FINISH_FINISH`, `START_FINISH`) and a `TimeLag`. It is widely assumed to be unused. The sharper rule, measured on one project delivered two ways, is that **design-tool exports carry nothing and 4D-tool exports carry the graph**.\n\nThe 48 MB ArchiCAD design model contained no `IfcTask` and no `IfcRelSequence`. The weekly as-planned models of the same building, exported by Synchro's IFC 4D exporter, contained **125 `IfcTask` and 42 `IfcRelSequence`** (41 `FINISH_START`, 1 `START_START`) with real activity names \u2014 setting out, pour, prefab stairs, calcium-silicate blockwork.\n\nSo the dependency edge is not missing from the industry. It is trapped in 4D exports that nobody queries, which is a materially different problem and a much cheaper one to solve.\n\nTwo caveats bound what can be built on it. **0 of 125 tasks carried `IfcTaskTime`**, so scheduled start, finish and duration are absent; the sequence topology travels and the dates do not, and anything date-dependent needs another source. And edges frequently run from an activity to itself by name (`Maatvoering \u2192 Maatvoering`), because the exporter emitted per-zone task instances rather than a clean activity network, so deduplication or zone-awareness is required before the graph reads sensibly.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T16:00:00.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-17T09:58:37.025Z"
    }
  },
